{"subjectId":"c","id":"file-io","title":"File I/O","group":"Files, Preprocessor & Program Organisation","order":47,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>&lt;stdio.h&gt;</code> works with <em>streams</em> (<code>FILE *</code>): open with <code>fopen</code>, read/write with the same family of functions you use for the console, and always <code>fclose</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock-open\"></i>Open modes</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Meaning</th><th>If file exists</th><th>If missing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>\"r\"</code></td><td>Read</td><td>Read from start</td><td>Fails (<code>NULL</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"w\"</code></td><td>Write</td><td><strong>Truncated to 0</strong></td><td>Created</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"a\"</code></td><td>Append</td><td>Writes go to the end</td><td>Created</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"r+\"</code></td><td>Read + write</td><td>Kept</td><td>Fails</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"w+\"</code></td><td>Read + write</td><td>Truncated</td><td>Created</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"a+\"</code></td><td>Read + append</td><td>Kept, writes at end</td><td>Created</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"…b\"</code></td><td>Binary (<code>\"rb\"</code>, <code>\"wb\"</code>)</td><td>No newline translation (matters on Windows)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>\"wx\"</code> (C11)</td><td>Exclusive create</td><td>Fails</td><td>Created</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pencil\"></i>Writing and reading text</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    FILE *f = fopen(\"scores.txt\", \"w\");\n    if (f == NULL) { perror(\"fopen\"); return 1; }\n    fprintf(f, \"asha 91\\nravi 78\\nmeera 85\\n\");\n    fclose(f);\n\n    f = fopen(\"scores.txt\", \"r\");\n    if (!f) { perror(\"fopen\"); return 1; }\n    char name[32];\n    int score, total = 0, count = 0;\n    while (fscanf(f, \"%31s %d\", name, &amp;score) == 2) {   /* loop on the read, not on feof */\n        printf(\"%-6s %d\\n\", name, score);\n        total += score;\n        count++;\n    }\n    fclose(f);\n    printf(\"average %.1f\\n\", (double)total / count);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>asha   91\nravi   78\nmeera  85\naverage 84.7</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-text-wrap\"></i>Line by line with fgets, appending</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    FILE *f = fopen(\"log.txt\", \"w\");\n    if (!f) return 1;\n    fputs(\"first\\n\", f);\n    fclose(f);\n\n    f = fopen(\"log.txt\", \"a\");             /* keep existing content */\n    if (!f) return 1;\n    fputs(\"second\\n\", f);\n    fputs(\"third\\n\", f);\n    fclose(f);\n\n    f = fopen(\"log.txt\", \"r\");\n    if (!f) return 1;\n    char line[128];\n    int n = 0;\n    while (fgets(line, sizeof line, f)) {\n        line[strcspn(line, \"\\n\")] = '\\0';\n        printf(\"%d: %s\\n\", ++n, line);\n    }\n    fclose(f);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1: first\n2: second\n3: third</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary\"></i>Binary files: fwrite / fread / fseek</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct { int id; double price; } Item;\n\nint main(void) {\n    Item items[3] = {{1, 9.5}, {2, 20.0}, {3, 4.25}};\n    FILE *f = fopen(\"items.bin\", \"wb\");\n    if (!f) return 1;\n    size_t written = fwrite(items, sizeof items[0], 3, f);\n    fclose(f);\n\n    f = fopen(\"items.bin\", \"rb\");\n    if (!f) return 1;\n    fseek(f, 0, SEEK_END);\n    long size = ftell(f);                          /* file size in bytes */\n    printf(\"wrote %zu records, file is %ld bytes\\n\", written, size);\n\n    Item it;\n    fseek(f, 2 * (long)sizeof(Item), SEEK_SET);   /* random access: jump to record #2 */\n    if (fread(&amp;it, sizeof it, 1, f) == 1) printf(\"record 2: id=%d price=%.2f\\n\", it.id, it.price);\n    rewind(f);\n    if (fread(&amp;it, sizeof it, 1, f) == 1) printf(\"record 0: id=%d\\n\", it.id);\n    fclose(f);\n    remove(\"items.bin\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>wrote 3 records, file is 48 bytes\nrecord 2: id=3 price=4.25\nrecord 0: id=1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Raw <code>fwrite</code> of structs is not portable between machines: padding, endianness and type sizes differ. For interchange formats write fields explicitly in a fixed byte order.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Character-level copy</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint copy_file(const char *src, const char *dst) {\n    FILE *in = fopen(src, \"rb\");\n    if (!in) return -1;\n    FILE *out = fopen(dst, \"wb\");\n    if (!out) { fclose(in); return -1; }\n    int c;\n    long bytes = 0;\n    while ((c = fgetc(in)) != EOF) { fputc(c, out); bytes++; }\n    int err = ferror(in) || ferror(out);\n    fclose(in);\n    fclose(out);\n    return err ? -1 : (int)bytes;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    FILE *f = fopen(\"a.txt\", \"w\");\n    if (!f) return 1;\n    fputs(\"copy me\\n\", f);\n    fclose(f);\n    printf(\"copied %d bytes\\n\", copy_file(\"a.txt\", \"b.txt\"));\n    printf(\"missing source -&gt; %d\\n\", copy_file(\"nope.txt\", \"c.txt\"));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>copied 8 bytes\nmissing source -&gt; -1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>fgetc</code> / <code>fputc</code></td><td>One character</td></tr><tr><td><code>fgets</code> / <code>fputs</code></td><td>One line / string</td></tr><tr><td><code>fscanf</code> / <code>fprintf</code></td><td>Formatted text</td></tr><tr><td><code>fread</code> / <code>fwrite</code></td><td>Blocks of bytes (binary)</td></tr><tr><td><code>fseek</code> / <code>ftell</code> / <code>rewind</code></td><td>Move / query the file position</td></tr><tr><td><code>feof</code> / <code>ferror</code> / <code>clearerr</code></td><td>Why did the last read stop?</td></tr><tr><td><code>fflush</code></td><td>Push buffered output to the OS</td></tr><tr><td><code>remove</code> / <code>rename</code> / <code>tmpfile</code></td><td>File-system helpers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>while (!feof(f)) { fgets(…); … }</code> processes the last line twice: <code>feof</code> only becomes true <em>after</em> a read has failed. Loop on the return value of the read function instead.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between text and binary mode?</summary><div><p>On POSIX there is none. On Windows, text mode translates <code>\\n</code> to <code>\\r\\n</code> on write (and back on read) and may treat Ctrl-Z as end-of-file; binary mode passes bytes through unchanged. Always use <code>\"rb\"</code>/<code>\"wb\"</code> for non-text data.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why must you call fclose?</summary><div><p>It flushes buffered data to the file (without it, the last writes may be lost if the program crashes or calls <code>_exit</code>), releases the file descriptor (processes have a limited number) and frees the <code>FILE</code> structure. <code>exit</code> and returning from <code>main</code> close open streams, but relying on that hides bugs.</p></div></details></section>"}